WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.222
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEFL preparation.

2
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:09.571
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.871 --> 00:00:17.736
Unit seventeen: Speaking: Take an Interview. Today you will answer interview questions fluently in about forty-five seconds.

4
00:00:18.036 --> 00:00:20.514
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.990
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:26.851
Interview.

7
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.952
Interview means a set of questions asked by one person.

8
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:32.714
Take the interview.

9
00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:35.841
Your turn. Say: Interview.

10
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:39.643
Interview.

11
00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:41.303
Familiar.

12
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:44.061
Familiar means well known to you.

13
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.371
The questions are familiar.

14
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:49.357
Your turn. Say: Familiar.

15
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.178
Familiar.

16
00:00:53.917 --> 00:00:54.674
Fluent.

17
00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:58.403
Fluent means smooth and natural when speaking.

18
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:00.250
Speak fluently.

19
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.212
Your turn. Say: Fluent.

20
00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:06.963
Fluent.

21
00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:08.625
Develop.

22
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:11.941
Develop means to explain in more detail.

23
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:14.642
Develop your answer with an example.

24
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.763
Your turn. Say: Develop.

25
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:21.439
Develop.

26
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:23.010
Reason.

27
00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:26.376
Reason means why you think or do something.

28
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:28.147
Give a reason.

29
00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.031
Your turn. Say: Reason.

30
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:34.856
Reason.

31
00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:36.641
Example.

32
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.252
Example means a specific case that supports an idea.

33
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.095
Add an example.

34
00:01:43.683 --> 00:01:46.812
Your turn. Say: Example.

35
00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:50.633
Example.

36
00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:52.199
Pause.

37
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.357
Pause means a short stop in speech.

38
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.659
A short pause is fine.

39
00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.542
Your turn. Say: Pause.

40
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.076
Pause.

41
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:05.632
Timer.

42
00:02:06.617 --> 00:02:08.906
Timer means a clock that counts time.

43
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:10.715
Watch the timer.

44
00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:13.470
Your turn. Say: Timer.

45
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:17.230
Timer.

46
00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:21.696
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:25.176
Visal, how does Take an Interview work?

48
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:38.479
A virtual interviewer asks you three or four questions about familiar topics. You have no preparation time and about forty-five seconds for each answer.

49
00:02:39.583 --> 00:02:41.653
How do I fill forty-five seconds?

50
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:54.307
Use a simple pattern: answer directly, give a reason, and add an example or a result. That is usually about five or six sentences.

51
00:02:55.417 --> 00:02:57.091
What if I make a mistake?

52
00:02:58.183 --> 00:03:08.234
Keep going. A quick self-correction is natural. Long silences, one-word answers and memorised speeches are the real problems.

53
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.330
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:12.183 --> 00:03:16.075
Grammar focus: Strategy: answer, reason, example.

55
00:03:16.375 --> 00:03:17.609
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:21.570
I prefer studying in the morning because I feel more focused.

57
00:03:22.483 --> 00:03:25.830
For example, I finished my homework before breakfast last week.

58
00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:34.098
Answer the question in your first sentence, give a reason, and add an example. Keep speaking naturally until the timer ends.

59
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:39.116
Be careful! Don't say: I stay silent for ten seconds.

60
00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:43.449
Say: I answer at once and keep speaking.

61
00:03:43.749 --> 00:03:45.424
No long silence.

62
00:03:46.233 --> 00:03:48.897
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:50.957
A Short Interview.

64
00:03:51.257 --> 00:03:54.011
The interviewer asks when Piseth likes to study.

65
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.127
He answers that he prefers the morning.

66
00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:00.597
He says he feels more focused and calm.

67
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.041
He gives an example from the previous week.

68
00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:07.660
He finishes just before the timer ends.

69
00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:12.780
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:13.617 --> 00:04:15.883
You get about blank seconds per answer.

71
00:04:16.183 --> 00:04:20.382
A, five. B, three hundred. C, forty-five.

72
00:04:23.933 --> 00:04:26.328
The answer is C: forty-five.

73
00:04:27.483 --> 00:04:29.216
Do you get preparation time?

74
00:04:29.516 --> 00:04:32.844
A, no. B, ten minutes. C, one hour.

75
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.970
The answer is A: no.

76
00:04:39.117 --> 00:04:41.766
A good pattern is answer, reason and blank.

77
00:04:42.066 --> 00:04:46.916
A, question. B, example. C, apology.

78
00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:52.362
The answer is B: example.

79
00:04:53.517 --> 00:04:55.679
When does Piseth prefer to study?

80
00:04:55.979 --> 00:04:59.470
A, at night. B, never. C, in the morning.

81
00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:05.265
The answer is C: in the morning.

82
00:05:06.417 --> 00:05:07.801
What is a real problem?

83
00:05:08.101 --> 00:05:13.078
A, long silence. B, a quick self-correction. C, a short pause.

84
00:05:16.633 --> 00:05:19.399
The answer is A: long silence.

85
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:23.810
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:27.961
How do you answer an interview question in forty-five seconds?

87
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.366
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:38.666 --> 00:05:52.130
I answer the question in my first sentence. Then I give a reason and an example, which usually fills about forty-five seconds. If I make a mistake, I correct it quickly and keep speaking.

89
00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.581
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:55.483 --> 00:06:01.131
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: answer, reason, example.

91
00:06:01.431 --> 00:06:05.372
Today's value is confidence. Speak up and be proud of who you are.

92
00:06:05.672 --> 00:06:08.417
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

93
00:06:08.717 --> 00:06:11.602
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

